<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Madras State</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1947–1969</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a> State was the successor of the Madras Presidency in independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, a province from 1947 and a state of the Republic from 1950. It lost its Telugu districts to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-state">Andhra State</a> in 1953 and Malabar to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a> in 1956, while gaining the Tamil-speaking southern taluks of Travancore-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kochi">Cochin</a>. In 1969 it was renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/madras-state.jpg" alt="Madras State" width="320" height="387" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Milenioscuro · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Madras_in_India_(1951).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
